{"text": "Conservative\nConsumer\nIdeas Floated\n\nCoalition Calls for\nLess Regulation\n\nBy Warren Brown\nWashington Post Staff Writer\n\n4441\n\nSlade AE seme 93s\nThe White House yesterday agreed\nto review a set of economic policy\nrecommendations put fourth by con-\nservative public interest groups that\nare mounting a challenge to the tradi-\ntional notion of consumerism.\nThe proposals, prepared by the\nnewly formed National Consumer\n<piltion, were received by White\nouse Chief of Staff John H. Sunu-\n_Du\u2014an act that pigdeel Shela cane\nsumer organizations whose leaders\nsay they have yet to get access to\nthe Bush administration.\nSununu is the former chairman of\nSs a major organizer of\n 33-group coalition whose mem-\nbers include the Heritage Foundation,\nAccuracy in Media, the Competitive\nEnterprise Institute and Citizens\n\u2018 Against Government Waste.\nThe coalition\u2019s ideas run in direct\ncontrast to the consumerism philoso-\nphy developed under the leadership of\nRalph Nader, which places a heavy\nemphasis on government regulation\nto reduce and eliminate business mis-\nbehavior in the marketplace.\nThe conservatives, who Nader this\nweek called \u201ca clutch of perpetrators\nmasquerading as consumer advo-\ncates,\u201d think that deregulation in\nmost cases is the best way to serve\n\nconsumers. The coalition\u2019s recom-\n\nmendations call for abolition of federal\nfuel economy standards, review of\nU.S. antitrust laws, elimination of\nfarm subsidies and U.S, trade barri-\ners, assessment of user fees for cer-\ntain government services and a host\nof other actions the coalition believes\nwill benefit consumers by removing\nrestrictions from the marketplace. ;\nSununu, who addressed a meeting\n| of the coalition yesterday, seemed to\nendorse the spirit, if not the content,\nof the voluminous policy proposals.\n\u201cTo a great extent, we have seen a\nphilosophy in Washington that one or\ntwo people locked inside of the Belt-\nway know what is best for the con-\nsumer,\u201d said Sununu. But there is \u201cno\nreason to believe that American citi-\nzens are not smart enough or capable\nenough to make decisions for them-\nselves,\u201d he said.\nConsumer groups including Public\nCitizen, the National Insurance Con-\nsumers Organization and the Center\nfor Auto Safety criticized the conser-\nvative coalition and Sununu\u2019s recep-\ntion of its proposals. In a letter to\nPresident Bush, the liberal groups\nsaid Sununu\u2019s participation in the co-\nalition\u2019s event \u201cwould send the wrong\nmessage to business, to government\nofficials and to all businesses.\u201d\nIn a related matter yesterday,\nPublic Citizen spokesman Bob Drey-\nfuss criticized coalition officials for\naccusing his group of taking \u201ckick-\nbacks\u201d in Public Citizen\u2019s evaluation\nlate last year of companies produc-\ning home radon-testing equipment,\nTwo of the companies that Public\nCitizen rated best in its evaluation\nhave paid the consumer group 50\ncents per sale of each testing kit.\nDreyfuss said total contributions to\nPublic Citizen from the companies\nhave amounted to about $400 so far,\n\nThe contributions were voluntary\nwith the evalu- |\n\nand had nothing to do\nation results, he said.",
